solutions.latex:
\exfigure{339_q5_s1}

The integral is the area between $y=|x-1|$ and the $x$-axis: a triangle for $-2\le x\le 1$ (base $3$, height $3$) and a triangle for $1\le x\le 2$ (base $1$, height $1$).

$$\begin{aligned} \int_{-2}^{2}|x-1|\,dx &= \dfrac{1}{2}\times 3\times 3+\dfrac{1}{2}\times 1\times 1 \\ &= \dfrac{9}{2}+\dfrac{1}{2} \\ &= 5 \end{aligned}$$

\exfigure{402_q23_s1}

$|2x-6|=0$ at $x=3$, so the graph is a V with vertex $(3,0)$. The integral is the area of two triangles, one on each side of $x=3$: the left one has base $2$ and height $|2(1)-6|=4$, the right one has base $3$ and height $2(6)-6=6$.
$$\begin{aligned}\int_1^6|2x-6|\,dx&=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot2.4+\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot3.6\\ &=4+9\\ &=13\end{aligned}$$

